  "content": {
    "id": "p12-q8-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "8",
    "prompt": "A line parallel to the $x$-axis cuts the graph of $y=x^2+2x-3$ at points $A$ and $B$. Given that $AB=6$, find the coordinates of $A$ and $B$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The points are $(-4,5)$ and $(2,5)$, in either order.\n\nTheir midpoint lies on the axis of symmetry $x=-1$.",
      "worked": "The axis of symmetry is\n$$\\begin{align*}x&=-\\frac{2}{2(1)}\\\\x&=-1.\\end{align*}$$\nSince $AB$ is horizontal, its midpoint lies on the axis of symmetry. Each endpoint is $\\frac{6}{2}=3$ units from this axis, so their $x$-coordinates are\n$$\\begin{align*}x_A&=-1-3=-4,\\\\x_B&=-1+3=2.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstituting gives\n$$\\begin{align*}y_A&=(-4)^2+2(-4)-3\\\\y_A&=5,\\\\y_B&=2^2+2(2)-3\\\\y_B&=5.\\end{align*}$$\nThus the points are $(-4,5)$ and $(2,5)$, in either order.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 46
  },
